      "limitations": [
        "Race is estimated, not recorded. Maryland's voter file has no race or ethnicity field. Each voter's race is estimated from their surname, first and middle names and ZIP code with Bayesian Improved Surname Geocoding (BISG, the wru package). The estimate for one voter is a set of probabilities, never a fact about that person.",
        "Counts are expected values: the sum of the probabilities for each group, rounded. They describe groups of voters and are most reliable for large groups. A cell of 1 to 9 is hidden like any other.",
        "BISG is known to err in a pattern. In integrated areas it tends to place Black voters in the white group, and it identifies multiracial voters, Native Americans and Pacific Islanders poorly: all of them land in 'other' or in another group. Hispanic and Asian estimates rest heavily on surname.",
        "ZIP code is coarser than a census block, so the geographic part of the estimate is weaker than in studies that geocode addresses. Voters with no usable ZIP code are placed using their county.",
        "The estimate does not use party, age or sex, so the race by party and race by age tables do not repeat what the model was given. Active and inactive registrants are both counted.",
        "The comparison with the Census is rough: it sets the estimated race of registered voters against the 2020 voting-age population, and registration rates differ by group."
      ]
    },
